<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belshazzar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 539 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belshazzar was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> as regent for about a decade while his father lived at the Arabian oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a>. In the Book of Daniel he is the king who sees the writing on the wall at a feast on the night Babylon falls. He probably died when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took the city in 539 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belshazzar.jpg" alt="Belshazzar" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabonidus_chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Belshazzar, in Babylonian Bēl-šar-uṣur, meaning &quot;Bel, protect the king&quot;, was the son and crown prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>. Through his mother he might have been a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, though this is not certain, and the claim may come from royal propaganda.</p><p>Belshazzar played a central part in the coup that overthrew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labashi-marduk">Labashi-Marduk</a> and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> to power in May 556 BCE. As the main beneficiary, inheriting the estates of Labashi-Marduk and of the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neriglissar">Neriglissar</a>, he was probably the chief orchestrator. Afterwards he appears as a prominent businessman and head of a wealthy household, owning lands throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in May 553 BCE to campaign in the west, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> his provisional seat and did not return until 543 or 542 BCE. Belshazzar administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in his absence. He could grant royal privileges, command part of the army and receive offerings. Yet he was always styled crown prince, never king, documents were dated by Nabonidus's years, and he was not allowed to preside over the New Year festival, which was not held while Nabonidus was away.</p><p>The main sources are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> and the Verse Account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, all written after the Persian conquest and biased in favour of Cyrus. When Cyrus invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in 546 BCE, Belshazzar took up a defensive position but sent no aid. On his return Nabonidus removed him from his administrative duties and dismissed officials he had appointed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Belshazzar helped defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> against Cyrus in 539 BCE. After the Persian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> the army entered Babylon without a fight on 12 October. His fate is not recorded. It is often assumed that he was killed when the city fell, but he may have died at Opis, or been captured and executed, or exiled with his father.</p><p>In the Book of Daniel, which scholars regard as historical fiction written about four centuries after his time, Belshazzar is king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and holds a feast using the vessels of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>. A hand writes on the wall, Daniel reads it as a judgment, and Belshazzar is killed that night. The book errs in calling him king and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>, and its Darius the Mede is unknown to history. The later authors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> and the Midrash presented him as a tyrant who oppressed his Jewish subjects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belshazzar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last king, Nabonidus, lacked dynastic legitimacy and added religious alienation to it: he favoured the moon god Sin over Marduk and spent roughly a decade at the Arabian oasis of Tayma while his son <b>Belshazzar</b> governed in Babylon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.5,44.5&amp;year=-549" aria-label="Babylonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Belshazzar"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6035 4720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6035 4720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.5,44.5&amp;year=-549">What was here in 549 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
